<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Túpac Amaru Revolutionary Movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1982">1982</a>–<a href="/years/1997">1997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as MRTA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru">Túpac Amaru</a> Revolutionary Movement (MRTA) was a Marxist–Leninist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> group active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> from the early 1980s, smaller than and rival to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a>. It is best known for seizing the Japanese ambassador's residence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in December 1996 and holding dozens of hostages for four months, until Peruvian commandos stormed the building in April 1997, killing all fourteen rebels. The group never recovered from that defeat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-revolutionary-movement.jpg" alt="Túpac Amaru Revolutionary Movement" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_MRTA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Túpac Amaru Revolutionary Movement, in Spanish Movimiento Revolucionario Túpac Amaru or MRTA, was formed between 1980 and 1982 by the merger of the Revolutionary Socialist Party (Marxist-Leninist) and MIR El Militante, the militant faction of the Revolutionary Left Movement. The first gathered former members of the armed forces who had served under the leftist government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-velasco-alvarado">Juan Velasco Alvarado</a>, and the second came from a Castroist guerrilla faction defeated in 1965.</p><p>It took its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a>, the eighteenth-century rebel who led a revolt against the Spanish from 1780 to 1782 and took his name from the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> ruler, executed in 1572. Its declared aims were to show leftist groups the viability of radical revolution and to offer an alternative to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a>. It was led by Víctor Polay Campos until his sentencing to 32 years in prison in 1992, and then by Néstor Cerpa Cartolini.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its first action was a bank robbery in La Victoria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, on 31 May 1982, in which the committee member Jorge Talledo Feria was killed by friendly fire. On 28 September 1984 members disguised as police fired on the United States Embassy, causing damage but no casualties. The group's attempt to expand into the countryside brought it into conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a>, which was stronger there, and it remained largely urban and middle-class. Counterterrorist measures, infighting, the imprisonment or deaths of senior leaders and the loss of leftist support weakened it further.</p><p>Its last major action was the hostage crisis at the Japanese ambassador's residence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>. In December 1996 fourteen members occupied the residence and held 72 hostages for more than four months. In April 1997, on the orders of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberto-fujimori">Alberto Fujimori</a>, the armed forces stormed the building, rescuing all but one of the remaining hostages and killing all fourteen militants. Later revelations suggested that at least three, and perhaps as many as eight, were executed after surrendering. The episode ended the movement as a threat to the Peruvian state and effectively dissolved it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-south-africa">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a> found in its report of 2003 that the movement was responsible for 1.5 per cent of the deaths it investigated. The commission observed that, unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a>, the movement claimed its actions, wore uniforms and abstained from attacking the unarmed population, but also that it carried out assassinations, hostage-taking and systematic kidnapping. It argued that the movement's attempt to justify violence helped legitimise the repressive policies of the Fujimori government. In 2006 a Peruvian court found Polay guilty of nearly 30 crimes. The People's Democratic Front, a far-left organisation of former members and sympathisers, has claimed the movement's legacy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Túpac Amaru Revolutionary Movement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The conflict that followed, involving the Shining Path, the smaller <b>Túpac Amaru Revolutionary Movement</b> and the security forces, cost about 69,000 lives according to the Truth and Reconciliation Commission of 2003, most of them Quechua-speaking peasants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
